                                  \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167781" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167781" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167783" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167783" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\cos(x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n       