   \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167784"