$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167785" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167785" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\cos(x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167786" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167786" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nEarlier part 9:\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)\n$$\n\nThe order of this differential equation is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0aa3c2b2bcb3b53287e69e3e5d32ee83ff40f201c7d3f7b17f2b7db61575242"} {"question" "q-167787" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5004826172de3368d6f8834a4b58955139d2ad087249ca3682c2f70cc6ff855"} {"question" "q-167787" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/state.json" "file_sha256" "c50048